# hal0 — privileged seam grant for running the FastFlowLM CLI (`flm list`,
# `flm pull`) inside the pinned `flm` runner image (#2993, ADR-0036).
#
# FLM pulls and the FLM catalogue probe run in the same rootful image the NPU
# slot serves with, against the same store mount, so the host FastFlowLM .deb
# version no longer decides which weights land in the store. Slots and their
# images live in ROOT's podman store; hal0-api runs as the unprivileged `hal0`
# user, so it delegates exactly these verbs to /usr/lib/hal0/bin/hal0-flmctl:
#
#   exec-list <token>               flm list -j in the running hal0-slot-<token>
#   run-list <image> <store>        flm list -j in a one-shot container
#   run-pull <image> <store> <tag>  flm pull <tag> in a one-shot container
#   check-tag <tag>                 side-effect-free validator probe
#
# Every podman subcommand, flag and in-container argv word is a literal in the
# wrapper; each operand is validated ROOT-side before podman is invoked. The
# one-shot containers get no devices, capabilities or security options, and
# `--pull=never` means nothing is pulled implicitly.
#
# Install (as root):
#   install -m 0755 -o root -g root hal0-flmctl /usr/lib/hal0/bin/hal0-flmctl
#   install -m 0440 -o root -g root hal0-flmctl /etc/sudoers.d/hal0-flmctl
#   visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/hal0-flmctl
#
# The grant is pinned to the helper binary and deliberately does not enumerate
# argv: the wrapper is the control surface, as for hal0-podman-ro/-rw.
# Revoke with: rm /etc/sudoers.d/hal0-flmctl. FLM pulls then fail with a clear
# error and the FLM catalogue falls back to the host flm binary.

hal0 ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/lib/hal0/bin/hal0-flmctl
